CORE
main.cpp

^\[main\.assertion\.1\] .* deallocated after throwing constructor: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] .* not deallocated: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] .* placement deallocation: SUCCESS$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
If the initialization of the object of a new-expression throws, the
matching deallocation function is called ([expr.new]/27-30): the usual one
for a non-placement allocation function, the placement one with the same
further parameter types, given the same placement arguments.
